#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""infra — the Factory: one production line for sending capacity (ROADMAP B144, plan 55 § 5.7).

The real path, automated: buy a domain → DNS and SPF/DKIM/DMARC → mailboxes → their credentials in the
toolbelt env by asset id → warm-up → registry rows `warming` → certification → `active` → the lane.
Demand decides *when* (`infra plan`), the registry decides *whether*, and Taylor's `go` authorises a
purchase until sixty clean days. Every step only a person can take is filed once in TAYLOR-TODO.md by
this tool, with its repeat key and the exact click.

  infra provision --domain D [--mailboxes N] [--provider P] [--prewarmed] [--go] [--dry-run] [--retry-purchase]
        Without --go: the PLAN (the calls it would make, what they cost) and nothing else.
        With --go: APPLY → VERIFY → COMMIT. Re-running the same line after a crash resumes from the last
        step that finished; a purchase whose outcome is unknown is never repeated without --retry-purchase.
  infra certify D [--dry-run]     every check, each PASS or FAIL with its reason; all pass → `active`
  infra seeds send D | --all      one probe from each mailbox of D to each seed inbox (INFRA_SEED_INBOXES)
  infra seeds read D | --all      where the probes landed (inbox or spam) and the receivers' SPF/DKIM/DMARC
  infra plan [--pool N] [--json]  what it would provision next and why; changes nothing
  infra tick [--dry-run] [--force]  B150, the daily timer (systemd/infra-tick.timer): certify every warming domain
                                  past its date; weekdays, the plan on INFRA_POOL_CMD (default `leads batch --stats
                                  --json`), the proposal in STATE/proposal.json for the morning message, and after
                                  sixty clean days with INFRA_AUTO_PURCHASE=1 the provision lines under the caps
  infra warmup [--domain D] [--go]  B150: Warmforge's warm-up by API (the Salesforge export) for mailboxes whose
                                  warm-up is still a filed click; needs INFRA_SALESFORGE_WORKSPACE and
                                  INFRA_WARMFORGE_WORKSPACE
  infra status [--json]           the halt flag, operations in flight, the Factory's rows, open human steps
  infra drain D --why "…"         no first letters; open threads finish (registry only)
  infra retire D                  mailboxes deleted, auto-renew off, rows retired; refused with open threads
  infra rollback ID               undo a provision that failed verify: release what can be released; never
                                  re-buys a domain (ID: the operation, provision-<domain>, or the domain)
  infra dns-txt D VALUE [--host H]   one TXT record through the provider (the Postmaster verification)
  infra contract PROVIDER --verdict PERMITTED --clause "…" --read DATE --word "who said so, when"
                                  the provider's contract verdict, on Taylor's word only (VISION #2)
  infra halt "why" | infra resume  the global stop: no new external mutation while halted
  infra doctor

The control plane (plan 55 § 5.7), which every later production line inherits:
  PLAN    prints the intended external mutations and their cost; changes nothing.
  APPLY   every external call is keyed (operation:step) and its ledger line is written *before* the call;
          a re-run finds the key, asks the provider what happened, and skips or resumes; never a second
          purchase.
  VERIFY  reads the world back (DNS answers, a login per mailbox) and never trusts the API's "ok".
  COMMIT  registry rows `warming`, the operation's ledger line (result and cost), a history line.
  A failed verify leaves the rows `provisioning` with the failure recorded; `infra rollback` compensates.

The global stop: `infra halt` (or FACTORY_HALT=1 in env) refuses every new external mutation — buy, DNS
write, mailbox create or delete, warm-up enrolment, a seed send — while status, plan, certify's checks,
the inbox and monitoring keep running. `infra resume` lifts the file flag (FACTORY_HALT in env is lifted
where it was set). Taylor can say "halt the factory" by text.

Env (names only; values are never printed):
  INFRA_PROVIDER              the default provider (default infraforge)
  INFRA_PROVIDER_API_KEY / INFRA_PROVIDER_API_BASE / INFRA_PROVIDER_WORKSPACE   the vendor's (TODO § 1)
  INFRA_USD_PER_DAY (50)      the most the Factory may commit to spending in a day
  INFRA_UNITS_PER_WEEK (2)    the most domains it may start provisioning in seven days
  INFRA_AUTO_PURCHASE=1       after sixty clean days, a purchase no longer waits on `--go`
  INFRA_MAILBOX_CAP (15)      a mailbox's first-touch day in `plan`'s arithmetic
  INFRA_POOL_CMD              a command printing the pool as a number, or JSON with INFRA_POOL_KEY (default
                              `qualify`); the tick's default is `leads batch --stats --json`
  INFRA_SALESFORGE_WORKSPACE / INFRA_WARMFORGE_WORKSPACE   warm-up by API (B150); unset, a filed click
  INFRA_SEED_INBOXES          our own seed inboxes, comma-separated (a Gmail, an Outlook, a Workspace
                              address), never given to the vendor
  INFRA_SEED_PASSWORD_<ADDR> / INFRA_SEED_IMAP_<ADDR>   each seed's app password and IMAP host:port
  INFRA_STATE / INFRA_LEDGER / TODO_FILE / CLAUDE_TOOLS_ENV / ASSETS_DB   where things live (tests)
  INFRA_NOW                   the clock (tests)

No business is named here (plan 55 § 5.12, tests/test_portable.py): the tenant, the sender's name and
everything else come from ventures/<name>/venture.toml (`--venture NAME`).
"""

